{{!-- One tab button, in whichever shape this sheet's layout wants.

      MODERN is the icon rail: a glyph worn as a CSS mask, name in the tooltip and the
      aria-label (see stonetop.tab-rail-item). CLASSIC is the horizontal strip: the name is
      the button. These two <a> shapes are the only chrome this toggle duplicates - the tab
      LIST itself, the stat block, the wounds line and the NPC quick facts are all one copy
      of the markup, mounted in two places, so the layouts cannot drift apart.

      `stonetop.classicLayout` is read off the INHERITED context rather than passed down: a
      partial's context is its caller's plus the hash, every nav sits at its template's root,
      and each of the three sheets puts its own effective value under that one key. So this
      partial needs no call-site plumbing and cannot be handed the wrong sheet's answer.

      The level-up dot is modern-only. In classic the vitals row is pinned above the tabs and
      actor-vitals.hbs already draws `.stonetop-levelup-icon` there with its own tooltip - the
      rail dot exists only because the redesign put the vitals behind a tab. The attention
      class is still stamped on the classic anchor so a classic-scoped dot rule is one
      selector away if that turns out to be wanted; no dot is drawn today. --}}
{{#if stonetop.classicLayout}}
<a class="item{{#if attention}} stonetop-tab-attention{{/if}}" data-tab="{{this.tab}}"
   aria-label="{{this.label}}{{#if attention}} ({{this.attentionLabel}}){{/if}}"
   {{#if attention}}data-tooltip="{{this.label}} ({{this.attentionLabel}})"{{/if}}><span class="stonetop-tab-label">{{this.label}}</span></a>
{{else}}
{{> "stonetop.tab-rail-item" tab=tab label=label attention=attention attentionLabel=attentionLabel}}
{{/if}}
